  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nToday, satellite imagery shows mostly clear skies over NH and Maine \nsouth of the mountains. Upslope flow over the mountains are allowing \ngravity waves to form, allowing for mostly cloudy skies north of the \nmountains. Gusty northwest winds are expected this afternoon, though \nthese winds will taper off overnight as high pressure briefly moves \noverhead. This brief period of calm conditions could allow for deep \nradiational cooling Tuesday night, especially south of the mountains \nwhere skies are clearer. Low temperatures could fall near freezing \neven along the coast.  Opted to issue Freeze Warnings for interior \nCumberland and Androscoggin counties, as these counties are most \nlikely to see below freezing temperatures due to exceptionally calm \nwinds. Kept the Frost Advisory elsewhere, as light winds may persist \nthrough the night in Augusta and along the immediate coast. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nHigh pressure passing New England to the south will allow for \nsouthwest return flow during the day on Wednesday. These winds could \nget quite breezy along the coast, with gusts of 25mph possible in \nCasco Bay and Midcoast. This should allow for a sharp rebound from \nthe near freezing temperatures likely to be seen in the morning. \nTemperatures look to be in the upper 50s across central Maine, with \nmid-60s elsewhere. A frontal passage will move across New England \nearly Thursday morning, with some showers possible with its passage. \nDespite the frontal passage, high pressure to the south will allow \nfor the continuation of southerly winds and the warmer temperature \ntrend into the day on Thursday. Temperatures look to be in the upper \n60s and lower 70s forecast then. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\n\nHigh pressure moves overhead for the weekend. Approaching high \npressure should allow for some cold air advection and northwesterly \nwinds on Friday. These winds will help keep temperatures seasonable \non Friday and Saturday.  The aforementioned high pressure system is \nprojected to move to the southeast on Saturday, allowing for warmer \nsoutherly return flow again. This return flow, and mid-level ridging \nis likely to bring above average temperatures on Sunday and into \nearly next week.\n\nModel guidance has also suggested a low pressure system could move \nin Monday afternoon and bring unsettled weather to the area.  A lot \nof uncertainty remains at this time, but it does appear that this \nsystem could be the next opportunity to see meaningful \nrainfall.",
